.TH ELLIPSETEST 1
.SH NAME
ellipsetest \- exercise memellipse's brushed path, which allocates a negative-origin image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ellipsetest
[
.I group
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ellipsetest
draws ellipses chosen to reach, or deliberately to miss,
.IR memellipse 's
brushed-with-circles path. That path is the only one in the draw library
that allocates an image with a
.B negative
.BR r.min.y :
.IR bellipse 's
brush disc,
.BR Rect(\-t,\-t,t+1,t+1) .
Two defects lived behind that, both reachable only from a kernel, so no
hosted run could show either.
.PP
.IR byteaddr (2)
computed its row offset as
.B (s32)(sizeof(u32)*p.y*i->width)
and
.B 7c
drops a narrowing cast of a 64-bit value, so a negative
.B p.y
left a nonzero high word on the pointer \(em an address gigabytes past
anything mapped, written to by
.IR memfillcolor .
.PP
.IR allocmemimage (2)
sized the disc with
.B nw = l*Dy(r)
in
.BR u32 ,
so a thickness whose true word count passed 2\u32\d wrapped to a small
one and the allocation came back far smaller than the rectangle it
described.
.PP
Reaching the brushed path needs
.IR a " != " b .
The criterion is
.B 2t(a\-b) > b\u2\d
for
.IR b " < " a ,
so a circle never gets there however thick it is, and neither does a
fill, whose
.B t
of \-1 makes the criterion false in both arms. The groups are therefore
.TP
.B brushed
three ellipses with
.IR a " != " b
and a thickness of 2, which do reach it. At
.IR a =44
and
.IR b =5
the criterion is 156 against 25.
.TP
.B plain
a circle and a fill at the same radii, which do not.
.TP
.B wrapping
the four thicknesses at which the word count wraps.
.PP
With no argument, or
.BR all ,
every group runs.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ellipsetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (3),
.IR gfxstress (1)
.SH BUGS
Two things here are deliberate and easy to undo by accident.
.PP
.B Every case flushes.
Draw operations are batched into the
.IR draw (3)
write buffer and executed when it is flushed, so without a flush per
case the count records how many messages were
.B queued
and the drawing all happens afterwards during teardown. An early version
of this test printed
.B "9 ok, 0 fail"
on a kernel that then died in
.IR memfillcolor
on its way out. A count that cannot fail is not a test.
.PP
.B The group is an argument rather than a loop
because console output is queued: a
.B print
and a trap print race, so the last line on the wire does not say which
case faulted. Selecting one group per boot attributes a fault by which
invocation dies, which does not depend on print ordering at all. The
bisection that separated the two defects above was done exactly that
way.
.PP
Nothing here checks pixels. A case proves a path was taken and survived,
not that it drew the right thing. It is also silent about
.I negative
semidiameters and thicknesses, which
.IR draw (3)
rejects on the way in, so they cannot reach the library at all \(em and
cannot reach the brushed path even if they did, since
.B a
and
.B b
are normalised at entry and a negative
.B t
makes both arms of the criterion false.
